{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>To a good land.</b> In which are gathered kinds of good things not gathered in any other region. He enumerates five kinds of good, and for each of them says 'a land.' The first is <b>a land of streams of water, springs and deeps</b> — not the waters of rivers and pools that are bad, the opposite of 'and the waters were bad and the land barren' (II Kings 2:19). The second is <b>a land of wheat and barley</b>, which are needed for sustenance. The third is <b>a land of olive oil and honey</b>, which are royal delicacies. The fourth is <b>a land in which you shall eat bread without scarcity</b> — that coin is plentiful and cheap there, as it says: 'and his land is filled with silver and gold, and there is no end to his treasures' (Isaiah 2:7). For indeed the dearness of coin is harder than the dearness of produce, as the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Ta'anit 19): 'they spoke only of a shortage of produce, but for a shortage of coin they sound the alarm immediately.' The fifth is <b>a land whose stones are iron</b> — in which are found many hard stones, bright, beautiful and good for building."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:20:55.679818275Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}